    {
      "duration_ms": 9000,
      "caption": "Two processes use the same virtual page number with different frames, so stale entries are wrong.",
      "narration": "Two processes can use the same virtual page number for different frames. Entries cached for one are wrong for the other.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Same VPN, different frames",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 8,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "ent",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "text": "VPN 0x400 -> PFN 0x91 (A)\nVPN 0x400 -> PFN 0x37 (B)",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 11.8,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "bad",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Without a tag, hardware cannot tell which entry belongs to the running address space.",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 50,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 15.2,
          "at": 3000,
          "enter": "fade",
          "color": "red"
        },
        {
          "id": "hl",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "bad",
          "at": 5500,
          "enter": "fade"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 9000,
      "caption": "Writing CR3 without PCID invalidates every non-global TLB entry.",
      "narration": "The simple fix is brutal. Without PCID, loading CR3 on a context switch drops all non-global entries, and the next accesses all miss.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Switch address space, lose the TLB",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 13,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "steps",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "mov cr3, next_pgd",
            "all non-global entries flushed",
            "every access misses and walks",
            "G-bit (global) entries survive"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 24,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 25.6,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "hl",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "steps",
          "at": 5500,
          "enter": "fade"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 10000,
      "caption": "PCID tags entries with a 12-bit ID in CR3[11:0], so a CR3 write can keep them.",
      "narration": "With CR4.PCIDE set, CR3's low twelve bits hold a PCID that tags TLB entries. Setting bit 63 on the write says do not flush.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "PCID: tag entries, keep them",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 8,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "cr3",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "text": "CR3 = pgd_pa | pcid\n      | (1ULL << 63)",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 11.8,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "n1",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Bit 63 = no-flush: the old entries stay, tagged with their PCID.",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 46,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 15.2,
          "at": 3500,
          "enter": "fade",
          "color": "green"
        },
        {
          "id": "n2",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Linux tracks only a handful of PCIDs per CPU (dynamic ASIDs), recycling the oldest.",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 64,
          "w": 84,
          "h": 15.2,
          "at": 6500,
          "enter": "fade",
          "color": "gray"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 10000,
      "caption": "arm64 does the same with an ASID in TTBR, and broadcasts invalidations in hardware.",
      "narration": "Arm64 tags entries with an ASID held in the TTBR. Invalidating other cores differs: x86 sends IPIs, arm64 broadcasts TLBI instructions.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "heading",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "ASIDs on arm64",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
